      "code_hash": "799ae3924d444855572a1b74edded076",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"part-a-frequency-response-measurements\">Part A: Frequency Response Measurements</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The goal of this part is to measure the induced secondary voltage as a function of frequency for three different cores:</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Air core (<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(V_{s0}||)</marimo-tex>)</li>\n<li>Ferrite core (<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(V_{s1}||)</marimo-tex>)</li>\n<li>Invar core (<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(V_{s2}||)</marimo-tex>)</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">This is used to determine the optimal operating frequency.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The induced EMF according to Faraday's Law:</span>\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||[\\epsilon = -N \\frac{d\\Phi}{dt}||]</marimo-tex><span class=\"paragraph\">For a sinusoidal current <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(I_1(t) = I_0 \\sin(\\omega t)||)</marimo-tex>, the induced secondary voltage is proportional to the angular frequency <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\omega = 2\\pi f||)</marimo-tex>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Load the frequency sweep measurements from the data acquisition software:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
